# Core efficiency

- For “pick up where we left off”: read `docs/ops/WORK_LOG.md` Active slice first, then implement that Jira story.
- For other non-trivial work: read `PROJECT_MEMORY.md` once at session start; do not re-dump it later.
- Prefer Ask or Plan before multi-file edits. One logical slice per chat when possible.
- Targeted reads and greps only. No whole-repo dumps. Prefer `/explorer-cheap` for discovery.
- Never store real credentials in the repo or chat. Local settings store only.
- No Jira writes / credential prompts until the user says a Jira account exists (KAN is live).
- Confirm before: security-level changes, deletes, credential updates, and non-routine Jira writes.
- **Exception (standing):** bugs found during development **must** be raised as Jira **Bug** tickets and Relates-linked to the relevant Story (+ test ticket) without waiting for a separate confirm. KAN types: Task=dev work, `test`=verification, Bug=defects. See AGENTS.md / work-log-handoff.
- Prefer small diffs; extend existing plugins — do not create parallel systems.
- After a finished slice: update Current State in `PROJECT_MEMORY.md` if behaviour changed.
- Classic orb styles stay pure canvas. **WebGL / Three.js is allowed** for the three new styles (mystic, aether, neural) only. Vendor Three.js under ui/vendor/ is acceptable for those styles.

# Model selection (cheapest first)

- Always start with the cheapest capable model: **Composer 2.5** or **Auto** (if available). Gemini 2.5 Flash is also acceptable for simple work.
- Do not use Grok 4.7, Claude Opus, or other frontier models unless the cheaper model has failed twice on the same task, or the task is explicitly marked as requiring a stronger model (e.g. visual orb matching against reference images).
- If you escalate, state why in one sentence before switching.
- Prefer the model picker to stay on Composer/Auto unless escalation is justified.

# Mode transitions (reduce token use)

- Auto-approve mode transitions are enabled. Use them.
- Start in **Ask** or **Plan** for exploration, design, or understanding the current code. These modes are lighter.
- Switch to **Agent** only when you are ready to make the actual edit.
- Use **Debug** mode for investigating failures instead of long free-form exploration in Agent mode.
- Prefer short Plan → Agent cycles over one long Agent session.
- If a task is purely investigative, stay in Ask/Plan and do not switch to Agent.

# Git handling (agents do this, never ask the user)

- You have terminal access. Run all git commands yourself.
- **Best moment to pull:** at the **start of a new Agent chat** / “pick up where we left off”, **before** reading tickets or coding. If not on `main`, stash, checkout `main`, then `git pull origin main`.
- Before committing: `git status` and `git diff`. Only commit the files you intentionally changed.
- **After every finished or paused slice:** commit with a clear message, then **`git push origin main`** (or the current branch if explicitly working on one). Unpushed finished work blocks the next pick-up chat.
- Never ask the user to run git, stash, pull, push, or “should I commit?” — including never ending a reply with “please `git pull`”. Agents sync the tree; the human only runs the desktop app when they want to look at it.
- If a pull would overwrite local work, stash first, pull, then decide whether to pop.

# Library docs gate (does not lose to thrift)

- When adding or changing calls to FastAPI, Pydantic, Playwright, Gradio, pywebview, Ollama HTTP API, or httpx: do **not** skip Context7 / installed-package docs to save tokens. One lookup per library per slice. See `AGENTS.md` and `.cursor/rules/tooling-use.mdc`.

